<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oltenia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lesser Wallachia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oltenia is the historical region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> and the Olt river, forming the western part of Wallachia. Its chief city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>, the traditional seat of the bans who governed it for the Wallachian princes. From 1718 to 1739 it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of Craiova, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-belgrade">Treaty of Belgrade</a> returned it to Wallachia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oltenia.jpg" alt="Oltenia" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manolemariaalina · CC BY-SA 3.0 ro · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valea_Oltului-Judetul_Valcea.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oltenia is a historical province in western Wallachia, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the Southern Carpathians and the Olt river, from which its name derives. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a> is its main city and traditional seat of power. The first medieval seat was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drobeta-turnu-severin">Turnu Severin</a>, anciently Drobeta, near the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>'s Bridge. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> it was called Vallachia Cisalutana, meaning Cis-Oltine Wallachia. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule it was officially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of Craiova and commonly Lesser Wallachia, and sometimes Imperial Wallachia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacians">Dacians</a> first inhabited the region, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> took in 106. In 129, under Hadrian, it formed Dacia Inferior, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>'s reform made it Dacia Malvensis, with its capital at Romula, colonised with legion veterans. The Romans withdrew their administration south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at the end of the 3rd century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, Taifals and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> ruled in turn. From 681 it was, with interruptions, part of the Bulgarian Empire. In 1233 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> formed the Banate of Severin in the west, which lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526.</p><p>Around 1247 a polity emerged under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litovoi">Litovoi</a>. When Wallachia arose in the 14th century, its voivode was represented in Oltenia by a ban, the Great Ban of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>, the highest office in the Wallachian hierarchy. The Craiovești family held it most from the late 15th century to about 1550, and the title lasted until 1831. Wallachia accepted Ottoman suzerainty in the 15th century; many bans cooperated with the Turks, while rulers such as the Oltenian-born Michael the Brave fought them.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> in 1718 Oltenia was split from Wallachia and annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>, though the Austrians held it de facto by 1716. It was the only part of the Danubian Principalities, apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a> later, to experience Enlightened absolutism. Welcomed at first, the Austrians lost goodwill by imposing administrative, fiscal and judicial reforms that withdrew privileges from nobles and enforced taxes on peasants. In 1761 the bans' residence moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, and in 1821 Oltenia was at the centre of Tudor Vladimirescu's uprising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Oltenia is part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and takes in the counties of Gorj and Dolj entirely and parts of Mehedinți, Vâlcea, Olt and Teleorman. Its traditional emblem, a gold lion holding a sword over a bridge, stands in the lower dexter quarter of the Romanian coat of arms; the bridge is that of Apollodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> at Drobeta Turnu Severin. An official Romanian administrative region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>, also called Oltenia, existed from 1952 to 1968.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oltenia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wallachia lies north of the Lower Danube and south of the Southern Carpathians, divided by the Olt River into Muntenia in the east and <b>Oltenia</b> in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Austro-Turkish war of 1716 to 1718 Habsburg forces occupied <b>Oltenia</b>, the part of Wallachia west of the Olt river.</blockquote><a href="/state/banat-of-craiova">Banat of Craiova</a></li><li><blockquote>The region passed to Wallachia as <b>Oltenia's</b> western edge.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-severin">Banate of Severin</a></li></ul></section>
